  /**
   * Update an individual feature (step) status
   *
   * <p>Set the status of a single verification feature (step) within a session. Target the feature by its workflow graph <code>nodeId</code>. On non-graph workflows, whose steps have no node, use <code>default</code> and also pass <code>feature_type</code> to say which step you mean.
   *
   * <p>Works for both User Verification (KYC) and Business Verification (KYB) sessions and every step type: ID/OCR, Liveness, Face Match, NFC, AML, Proof of Address, Phone, Email, Database Validation, Questionnaire, and the KYB registry / documents / key-people checks. This endpoint is <b>not</b> available for standalone API sessions (<code>session_type = API</code>).
   *
   * <p><code>new_status</code> must be one of <code>Approved</code>, <code>Declined</code>, or <code>In Review</code>, and must differ from the feature's current status.
   *
   * <p><b>When the override applies.</b> Without <code>defer_until_flow_finishes</code> (or with <code>false</code>) the override applies at once, as it always did; when the person is still in the flow it is recorded as <code>forced</code>. With <code>defer_until_flow_finishes: true</code> while the person is still in the flow (the session is <code>Not Started</code>, <code>In Progress</code> or <code>Resubmitted</code> and has not finished), the override is stored as <code>pending_until_flow_finishes</code> and applies when the flow finishes, after the flow reaches its own decision. On a session whose flow already ended it applies at once either way. A waiting override is cancelled instead when the person redoes its step before finishing, when a newer override on the same check replaces it, when the session leaves the flow unfinished (abandoned, expired, or decided mid-flow), or when you cancel it with <code>POST /v3/session/{sessionId}/features/overrides/{overrideId}/cancel/</code>. List a session's overrides with <code>GET /v3/session/{sessionId}/features/overrides/</code>.
   *
   * <p><b>Effect on the session.</b> A session that already has a decision keeps it: only the check changes. When the session has no decision yet and its workflow is waiting on exactly this step, applying the override completes the step: the workflow moves on, later steps run, and a status node can set the session decision and fire <code>status.updated</code>.
   *
   * <p><b>Webhooks and activity.</b> A <code>data.updated</code> webhook fires when the override applies (at once, or when the flow finishes), never while it waits or when it is cancelled. Every override is recorded in the session's activity as <code>FEATURE_OVERRIDE_PENDING</code>, <code>FEATURE_OVERRIDE_APPLIED</code> or <code>FEATURE_OVERRIDE_CANCELLED</code>, with who made it; the request's entry carries your <code>comment</code>.
   *
   * <p>Authenticate with your API key (<code>x-api-key</code>) or a user authorization header carrying the <code>write:sessions</code> privilege; a scoped API key needs write access to decisions.
   *
   * <p>{@code PATCH /v3/session/{sessionId}/features/{nodeId}/update-status/}
   *
   * @param sessionId UUID of the verification session. Accepts both user (KYC) and business (KYB) session IDs — the service resolves the ID across both session types.
   * @param nodeId Workflow graph node ID of the feature to update (for example `feature_ocr_1`). Use the literal value `default` for non-graph (single-step) workflows where the feature has no node ID.
   * @param params the request parameters
   * @param options per-request options that override the client options
   * @return the response body
   */
  public SessionsUpdateFeatureStatusResponse updateFeatureStatus(String sessionId, String nodeId, SessionsUpdateFeatureStatusParams params, RequestOptions options) {
    return client.execute(updateFeatureStatusRequest(sessionId, nodeId, params), options, UPDATE_FEATURE_STATUS_RESPONSE).data();
  }

